// Factions. Players are given them in this order (player 0, who starts in the west, the first; player 1,
// in the east, the second; and so on), so picking a faction on the start screen picks the player.
// experience (optional): the faction's units earn experience and evolve into the forms in units.json
// that evolve from them. levels lists the experience each level needs, in order; at each level a unit
// can take its next form. Units earn perDamage for each hit point of damage they deal to enemy units
// and buildings, perKill for each enemy unit they kill, perBuildSecond for each second of building, and
// perMinute for each minute they stay alive.
// uiLook (optional): how the menus, panels and buttons look while playing the faction: timber (rough
// planks), stone (castle brick) or crystal (dark slate); see game/scripts/UiStyle.cs. Only for looks.
// tamesMonsters (optional): the faction's units beat wild monsters instead of killing them, and the
// player then has each one ridden or recruited (see "lair" and "ride" in units.json). Other factions get
// a lair's reward for clearing it.
// den (optional): what the faction's players can make of a monster den once its lair is cleared, using it
// up: { "building": id } puts that building (one with "fromDen" in buildings.json) up on the den as a site
// to build, or { "form": id } has the nearest unit near the den that can take that form (one with "fromDen"
// in units.json) take it. Without it, the faction's players leave dens be.
// duskTree (optional): what the faction's players can do to the map's dusk tree once its warden is gone:
// heal (a unit that heals tends it, grows its canopy and takes up the Dusk Staff) or raze (any of their
// fighting units hack it down, so nobody gets the staff). See "Dusk tree" in docs/rules.md.
// aiStyle (optional): how a computer player plays the faction: waves (the default: it masses its soldiers
// and sends nearly all of them as one big wave, and builds no walls) or balanced (it walls its town, and
// when it attacks it keeps a good part of its soldiers at home behind the wall and sends the rest). See
// "Winning and computer players" in docs/rules.md for what each level makes of it.
[
  { "id": "settlers", "name": "Settlers", "uiLook": "stone", "description": "Humans who grow their units into new forms through experience.",
    "experience": { "levels": [150], "perDamage": 1, "perKill": 10, "perBuildSecond": 1, "perMinute": 5 },
    "den": { "form": "wolfheart" }, "duskTree": "heal", "aiStyle": "balanced" },
  { "id": "orcs", "name": "Orcs", "uiLook": "timber", "description": "Clans who tame and ride the monsters they defeat.", "tamesMonsters": true,
    "den": { "building": "wolf_lodge" }, "duskTree": "raze", "aiStyle": "waves" }
]
